Let me look at the `tls_parse_ctos_psk` function's loop more carefully. In the `else` branch (when `sess == NULL`):

```c
} else {
    uint32_t ticket_age = 0, now, agesec, agems;
    int ret;

    ret = tls_decrypt_ticket(s, PACKET_data(&identity),
                             PACKET_remaining(&identity), NULL, 0,
                             &sess);

    if (ret == SSL_TICKET_EMPTY) {
        SSLfatal(s, SSL_AD_DECODE_ERROR, SSL_F_TLS_PARSE_CTOS_PSK,
                 SSL_R_BAD_EXTENSION);
        return 0;
    }

    if (ret == SSL_TICKET_FATAL_ERR_MALLOC
            || ret == SSL_TICKET_FATAL_ERR_OTHER) {
        SSLfatal(s, SSL_AD_INTERNAL_ERROR,
                 SSL_F_TLS_PARSE_CTOS_PSK, ERR_R_INTERNAL_ERROR);
        return 0;
    }
    if (ret == SSL_TICKET_NONE || ret == SSL_TICKET_NO_DECRYPT)
        continue;
    ...
}
```

If `tls_decrypt_ticket` returns `SSL_TICKET_NO_DECRYPT` with `sess` pointing to freed memory (the `slen != 0` case), then `continue` is executed. In the next iteration, `sess != NULL` (dangling), so it enters the `if (sess != NULL)` branch.

But to trigger this, we need a valid ticket. 

